{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794987669,"nonce":"WYHRK3ceZ/LUrFwxsQ841Pakomk=","title":"[GitHub #82] Modal/dialog overlays: no way to choose which element receives focus when the overlay opens","message":"Source: https://github.com/project-blinc/Blinc/issues/82\nReporter: FreeSynergy\nGitHub state at capture: open\nReported: 2026-11-17T00:33:03Z; updated: 2026-11-17T00:33:03Z\nCaptured: 2026-11-18T07:38:25.958Z\n\nStatus: queued from the upstream report. This import does not claim local reproduction, implementation or a fix.\n\nTracking scope and expected outcome\n\nAllow an explicit initial-focus target when a modal/dialog opens, before the first-focusable fallback. Investigate ElementHandle::focus()/BlincContextState::set_focus routing and focus synchronization. Preserve modal trapping, valid fallback behavior and restoration on close. Related local accessibility umbrella: 26f0d53; keep this concrete focus/API gap separate from the desktop adapter work.\n\nOriginal GitHub report\n\n## Summary\n\nWhen a modal or dialog overlay opens, the focus scope moves keyboard focus into it (`crates/blinc_layout/src/renderer/keyboard_nav/focus_scope.rs:101`, `sync_keyboard_focus`; the fallback is around line 180). The target is fixed: the first focusable descendant, or the overlay root if there is none. There is no option, attribute or builder method to name another element, for example the safe button of a destructive confirmation, or a text field further down a form.\n\n`ElementHandle::focus()` / `BlincContextState::set_focus(Some(id))` look like the way to do it, but they only store the string id and call `focus_callback`; on current upstream main nothing calls `set_focus_callback` (defined at `crates/blinc_core/src/context_state.rs:716`; its doc says the windowed app does), so the call does not reach the event router. Even focusing B directly on the `EventRouter` before the overlay opens is overridden by `sync_keyboard_focus`.\n\n## Expected\n\nA way to declare the initial focus target of a modal/dialog, for instance a `data-autofocus` attribute (or `.autofocus()` on `Div`) honoured by `sync_keyboard_focus` before it falls back to the first focusable descendant, or a builder option on `ModalBuilder`/`DialogBuilder`.\n\n## Actual\n\nThe first focusable descendant always gets focus. For a \"Delete\" / \"Cancel\" dialog whose first stop is the destructive button, the user can only avoid that by reordering the DOM. Calling `handle.focus()` while opening has no effect on keyboard focus (see repro).\n\n## Minimal repro\n\nIntegration test in `crates/blinc_layout/tests/`. It uses the attributes `overlay_focus::scope` puts on a dialog (`data-focus-scope`, `data-focus-modal`, `tabindex=\"-1\"`) on a `Div` inside the overlay layer, so it needs no window.\n\n```rust\nuse blinc_core::context_state::{BlincContextState, HookState};\nuse blinc_layout::div::div;\nuse blinc_layout::event_router::EventRouter;\nuse blinc_layout::renderer::RenderTree;\nuse blinc_layout::widgets::overlay::OVERLAY_LAYER_ID;\nuse std::sync::{atomic::AtomicBool, Arc, Mutex};\n\n#[test]\nfn dialog_initial_focus_cannot_be_chosen() {\n    BlincContextState::init(blinc_core::reactive::global_graph(),\n        Arc::new(Mutex::new(HookState::new())), Arc::new(AtomicBool::new(false)));\n    let item = |id: \u0026str| div().id(id).w(80.0).h(30.0).attr(\"tabindex\", \"0\");\n    let dialog = div().w(200.0).h(100.0).attr(\"data-focus-scope\", \"dlg\")\n        .attr(\"data-focus-modal\", \"true\").attr(\"tabindex\", \"-1\")\n        .child(item(\"a\")).child(item(\"b\"));\n    let ui = div().w(400.0).h(300.0).child(div().id(OVERLAY_LAYER_ID).w(400.0).h(300.0).child(dialog));\n    let mut tree = RenderTree::from_element(\u0026ui);\n    tree.compute_layout(400.0, 300.0);\n    let mut router = EventRouter::new();\n    let (a, b) = (tree.query_by_id(\"a\").unwrap(), tree.query_by_id(\"b\").unwrap());\n    // Wish: B gets the start focus. The only public ways:\n    BlincContextState::get().set_focus(Some(\"b\")); // what ElementHandle::focus() calls\n    router.set_focus(Some(b));                       // direct router call\n    tree.sync_keyboard_focus(\u0026mut router);          // what the frame loop does on open\n    println!(\"focused: a={} b={}\", router.focused() == Some(a), router.focused() == Some(b));\n    assert_eq!(router.focused(), Some(a), \"focus is on A, not the wished B\");\n}\n```\n\nMeasured output: `focused: a=true b=false` (test passes, i.e. focus is on A). Removing the `router.set_focus(Some(b))` line gives the same result.\n\n## Suggested direction\n\nIn `sync_keyboard_focus`, before picking `candidates.first()`, look for a descendant carrying an opt-in attribute such as `data-autofocus` and prefer it. Optionally wire `set_focus_callback` in the windowed app so `ElementHandle::focus()` reaches the router for elements inside an open overlay.\n\nWe are happy to help with a pull request if you tell us which direction you prefer.\n\nAlso tracked in git-bug in our fork FreeSynergy/Blinc (id 9743496).","files":null}]}